 ## Overview
-Fine-tuning in 3D Gaussian Splatting refers to the phase of optimization where an initial set of Gaussian primitives is refined to minimize rendering error, often leveraging pretrained weights or specific initialization strategies to accelerate convergence and improve fidelity [S2]. It addresses the problem of sparse or noisy initial point clouds (e.g., from Structure-from-Motion) by adaptively densifying, splitting, and pruning Gaussians during the training loop [S2, S4]. It sets itself apart from static initialization methods by dynamically adjusting the Gaussian population based on rendering loss and gradient metrics, and from pure feed-forward generation by allowing iterative parameter optimization [S2].
+Fine-tuning in 3D Gaussian Splatting refers to the phase of optimization where an initial set of Gaussian primitives is refined to minimize rendering error, often leveraging pretrained weights or specific initialization strategies to accelerate convergence and improve fidelity [S2]. It addresses the problem of initializing Gaussian representations by generating them via a direct feed-forward process or refining existing sets through iterative optimization [S2]. It sets itself apart from static initialization methods by dynamically adjusting the Gaussian population based on rendering loss and gradient metrics, and from pure feed-forward generation by allowing iterative parameter optimization [S2].
 
 ## How it works
-The fine-tuning process typically begins with an initial set of 3D Gaussians $\mathcal{G}$, which may be generated via a feed-forward network or extracted from SfM data. The core mechanism involves iterative optimization of geometry ($\mu, \Sigma$), opacity ($\alpha$), and color attributes (SH coefficients $\mathbf{c}$) using differentiable rasterization [S2].
