 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $N$ and views $V$. Rendering is $O(N)$ per pixel, optimization is $O(N \cdot V)$ per iteration. Densification adds overhead but reduces total iterations needed for convergence [Parent].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing Gaussian attributes. Fine-tuning with densification can increase $N$ significantly, requiring more memory [Parent].
-*   **Performance:** Fine-tuning with pretrained weights accelerates convergence and improves photorealism [S2]. Rendering-guided densification improves map quality in SLAM [S3].
-*   **Strengths:** Adapts to scene complexity, handles sparse initialization, improves fidelity [S2, S4].
+*   **Performance:** Fine-tuning with pretrained weights accelerates convergence and improves photorealism [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Improves fidelity and adapts to scene complexity via feed-forward initialization or iterative refinement [S2][S4, unofficial].
 *   **Limitations:** Can suffer from forgetting in continuous mapping [S3], overfitting if not regularized [S5], and high memory usage with large $N$ [Parent].
-*   **When to Use:** When initial point cloud is sparse or noisy, when high fidelity is required, or when adapting to specific visual modes [S2, S3][S6, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and $N$ is already large, or when input data is already dense and high-quality [S4].
+*   **When to Use:** When high fidelity is required, when adapting to specific visual modes, or when refining feed-forward initialized Gaussians [S2][S4, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance is critical and $N$ is already large, or when input data is already dense and high-quality [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting builds on the original 3DGS method which introduced adaptive density control [Parent]. The concept of fine-tuning pretrained models for specific tasks is well-established in deep learning, where lower layers are fixed and higher layers are adapted [S1]. In 3DGS, fine-tuning strategies have been developed to address specific challenges such as sparse views [S1], blurry inputs [S1], and dynamic scenes [S1]. The method is widely used in novel view synthesis, SLAM, and 3D editing applications [S1, S2, S3].
